This repo contains the configuration that describes the content of the
`qcom-next` integration kernel tree, plus the CI that validates and publishes
it.

qcom-next is a deterministic function of its configuration:

    qcom-next = f( base.sha , ordered topics[].sha , rerere cache revision )

`qcom-next` is never hand-edited. The only way to change it is to change the
configuration through a reviewed, CI-gated pull request on this repository.


Source of truth
---------------

`qcom-next.yaml` is the single reviewed configuration. It records, for every
consumed topic branch, the exact SHA that was validated ("stable SHA"), the tag
pinning that SHA, and any inter-topic dependencies. It also records the upstream
baseline the topics are merged on top of.

`qcom-next.conf` is the legacy flat format consumed by automerge's `ci-merge`.
It is being retired: CI generates it from `qcom-next.yaml` as a disposable build
artifact. Do not edit it by hand and do not treat it as reviewed content.

Topic branch owners live in `topic_maintainers.json`, keyed by the topic `name`
used in `qcom-next.yaml`. Owner data is intentionally not duplicated into the
YAML.


Promoting a topic branch
------------------------

1. Tag the SHA you want consumed, on your topic branch:

       <branch>-<kernel-version>-<YYYYMMDD>

   for example `tech/bsp/clk-7.3-rc1-20260907`. Tagging matters: without a tag
   the consumed commit becomes unreachable the moment the topic branch is
   rebased.

2. Open a pull request against this repository that changes only your topic's
   `sha` and `tag` in `qcom-next.yaml`.

3. CI validates the config, classifies the change, generates a candidate
   qcom-next tree in which every other topic stays pinned at its current stable
   SHA, and runs the build and test gates against that candidate.

4. If the gates pass and the required reviewers approve, the publication
   workflow regenerates the tree, verifies it matches what was tested,
   force-pushes `qcom-next`, and merges your config PR. Your new SHA is now the
   stable SHA.

If the gates fail, the PR is blocked and the evidence is posted on it. Push a
fix and CI re-runs from the beginning.


Upreving the kernel RC
----------------------

An RC uprev changes only the `base` block and leaves every topic SHA untouched,
so that any new failure is attributable to the upstream kernel rather than to a
topic update. While an RC uprev is in flight, `promotion_state` is set to
`frozen`: topic promotion PRs may still run validation and CI, but must not
publish or merge until the state returns to `open`.

Do not mix the two kinds of change. A PR that edits both `base` and topic SHAs
is rejected as `invalid-mixed-change`, because a regression in it could not be
attributed to either side.


Merge conflicts
---------------

Conflicts are replayed from a shared git rerere cache. If tree generation hits a
conflict with no recorded resolution, CI fails and reports the conflicting
paths on the PR. The topic owner then runs automerge locally with the same
config and cache revision, resolves the conflict, and pushes the updated rerere
cache. CI re-runs from validation through the full gates.


Validating locally
------------------

Requires `pyyaml` and `jsonschema`:

    pip install pyyaml jsonschema

Structural checks only, no network:

    scripts/validate_qcom_next_config.py --offline

Full validation, including that every SHA and tag really exists on its remote
and that each tag resolves to its configured SHA:

    scripts/validate_qcom_next_config.py

Classify a change the way CI will, against the branch you will target:

    scripts/classify_qcom_next_change.py --base-ref origin/main

Run the tooling's own tests:

    scripts/selftest_qcom_next_config.py
    scripts/selftest_qcom_next_generate.sh
    scripts/selftest_qcom_next_ci.py

During the migration from `qcom-next.conf`, most topics do not yet have a
conventional tag at their pinned SHA. Pass `--allow-untagged` to downgrade those
errors to warnings. Promotion PRs are expected to run without that flag.


Generating the tree locally
---------------------------

CI generates the candidate tree, but the same script reproduces it locally,
which is what you need when resolving a merge conflict:

    scripts/generate_qcom_next_tree.sh --kernel-dir /path/to/kernel-topics

It writes the following to its `--out-dir`:

    merge.conf        generated flat conf for ci-merge (disposable)
    merge.log         full ci-merge output
    tested-set.txt    the inputs that define the tree, with a digest
    topic-shas.tsv    topic -> pinned SHA and merged commit count
    conflicts.txt     conflicting topics and paths, if any
    summary.md        Markdown summary

How the pinning works, since it is not obvious: automerge's `ci-merge` normally
merges live branch tips. Two of its existing behaviours are used to feed it
pinned commits instead. It prefers a local ref named `override/<topic>` over
`<remote>/<branch>`, so creating that ref at the pinned SHA pins the topic. It
also takes the merge base from `remotes/<baseline>/<branch>`, and there is no
override hook for the baseline, so the generated conf points every row at the
local kernel worktree over `file://` after local branches there have been reset
to the pinned SHAs. That pins the baseline as well, and has the side benefit of
turning what would be 78 network fetches into local ones.

Consequently the generated tree depends only on the config, never on what a
topic branch tip happens to be at the time. The self-tests assert exactly that
by advancing every branch tip and requiring the regenerated tree to be
unchanged.

To confirm a regenerated tree is the one that was tested:

    scripts/check_tested_tree_matches.sh --expected tested-set.txt \
                                         --kernel-dir /path/to/kernel-topics

The digest deliberately covers the inputs and not the integration commit:
`ci-merge` stamps merge commits with the current time, so two runs from
identical inputs differ in commit id while consuming identical content.


What CI does with a config PR
-----------------------------

`.github/workflows/qcom-next-pr-ci.yml` runs on every PR that touches the
configuration:

    validate + classify
      -> generate the candidate tree from the pinned SHAs
      -> push it as a candidate ref
      -> dispatch the kernel, Yocto and Ubuntu gates against that ref
      -> report everything back on the PR

Candidate refs are named

    qcom-next-candidate/pr-<pr-number>-<short-sha>

and are currently pushed to `qualcomm-linux-stg/kernel`, a staging repository,
so the flow can be exercised without touching production refs. The target is
configurable through the `candidate_repo` dispatch input.
`.github/workflows/prune-qcom-next-candidates.yml` deletes them once their PR is
closed, or after seven days.

A `metadata-only` or `no-change` PR does not alter the generated tree, so the
build and test gates are skipped and only validation runs.

The gates are invoked through their existing `workflow_dispatch` interface
rather than as reusable workflows, because two of them read some inputs through
`github.event.inputs.*`, which is populated for a dispatch but empty under
`workflow_call`. Completion is detected by polling the commit status of the
candidate commit: each gate already reports through
`.github/actions/workflow_status`, which posts a commit status whose `context`
is the gate's check name. That is an exact correlation, needs no run-id
guessing, and keeps working when a gate is retried.

`qcom-next` itself is never written by this workflow. Publication is a separate,
serialized step.


Validation rules
----------------

    V1  config parses and conforms to schemas/qcom-next.schema.json
    V2  no duplicate topic names, no duplicate repo/branch pairs
    V3  every configured SHA exists and its tag resolves to that SHA
    V4  every configured topic branch still exists on its remote
    V5  a topic with sha: null resolves to the branch tip, to be recorded+tagged
    V6  a promotion PR changes only topics; an RC uprev changes only base
    V7  no promotion publishes while promotion_state is frozen
    V8  depends_on references real topics, has no cycles, and merge order
        respects the dependency graph

V1-V5 and V8 are checked by `scripts/validate_qcom_next_config.py`, which
examines one config in isolation. V6 and V7 are diff-based and are checked by
`scripts/classify_qcom_next_change.py`, which compares the PR config against the
target branch.


Inter-topic dependencies
------------------------

Declare them with `depends_on` in `qcom-next.yaml`. Two promotion styles are
supported:

  * Sequential - the dependency is promoted and merged first, then the dependent
    topic is promoted on top of it. Each change is tested in isolation, so
    regressions are attributed precisely.

  * Atomic - both topic SHAs move in the same PR and are tested together. Useful
    for tightly coupled changes, at the cost of less precise attribution.

A multi-topic PR is accepted only when `depends_on` connects the promoted
topics. Otherwise it is rejected as `invalid-mixed-change`; split it into
sequential PRs, declare the dependency, or ask the integration owner for an
override.


Change classifications
----------------------

CI labels every config change as exactly one of:

    rc-uprev                only base fields changed
    topic-promotion         exactly one topic's sha/tag changed
    atomic-topic-promotion  several dependency-linked topic SHAs moved together
    metadata-only           depends_on, comments, or other unpublished metadata
    rerere-only             only the rerere cache reference changed
    no-change               config is identical to the base
    invalid-mixed-change    rejected; see V6

`metadata-only` changes do not alter the generated tree and therefore need
validation only. Everything else requires the full gates.


Migration helper
----------------

`scripts/bootstrap_qcom_next_yaml.py` regenerated `qcom-next.yaml` from the
legacy `qcom-next.conf` during the initial migration, capturing each branch tip
as the first pinned SHA. It is kept in-tree so that migration is reproducible
and auditable; it is not part of the normal promotion flow.


Other files
-----------

`ci.conf.sample` is a sample automerge configuration if you want to run local
integration testing with your own branch list.
